          %h3#LeftKanExtensionPreservingCertainLimits Left Kan extensions preserving certain limits
          %p
            The following statement says that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+exact+functors"} left exact functors
            into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            have left exact left Kan extension along the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+extension"}> Yoneda extension
            ) and that this is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+toposes"} sheaf toposes
            if the original functor preserves
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covers"} covers
          %p
            (We state this in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            by just disregarding all occurrences of “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            ”.)
          #YonedaExtensionOfLeftExact.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
              be an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
              and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              be an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-site"} (∞,1)-site
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaf+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-sheaf (∞,1)-category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Sh
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(\mathcal{C})
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒳
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi Func
                      %mrow
                        %mi lex
                        %mo ,
                        %mi leftadj
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝒳
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi L
                    %mi Func
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi PSh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝒳
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi Y
                    %mi Func
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝒳
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Top(\mathcal{X}, Sh(\mathcal{C}))
                    \\simeq
                    Func^{lex, leftadj}(Sh(\mathcal{C}), \mathcal{X})
                    \\stackrel{(-)\circ L }{\longrightarrow}
                    Func(PSh(\mathcal{C}), \mathcal{X})
                    \\stackrel{(-)\circ Y }{\longrightarrow}
                    Func(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{X})
            %p
              given by precomposition with
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stackification"} ∞-stackification
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheafification"} sheafification
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              and with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Yoneda+embedding"} (∞,1)-Yoneda embedding
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} full and faithful (∞,1)-functor
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+image"} essential image
              consisist of those
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} (∞,1)-functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi 𝒳
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon \mathcal{C} \longrightarrow \mathcal{X}
              which are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+exact+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} left exact
              and which preserve covers in that for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \to X\}_i
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering"} covering
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi i
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_i f(U_i) \to f(X)
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+epimorphism"} effective epimorphism
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒳
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{X}
          %p
            This appears as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Lurie, HTT, prop. 6.2.3.20
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#YonedaExtensionOfLeftExact"}
              is a central statement in the theory of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+toposes"} classifying toposes
          %p
            For more discussion of left exactness properties preserved by left Kan extension see also (
            %a{:href => "#BorceuxDay"}> Borceux-Day
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#KarazerisProtsonis"} Karazeris-Protsonis
          %h3#AlongFibrations Kan extension along (op)fibration
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : C \to D
              be a small
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opfibration"} opfibration
              of categories, and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              be a category with all small
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in D
              the inclusion
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f^{-1}(d) \to f/d
            %p
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
              into the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
              given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Id
                      %mi d
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Id
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    c \mapsto (c, Id_{d} = Id_{f(c)})
            %p
              has a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi c
                    %mo ′
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (c, f(c) \to d) \mapsto c'
                    \\,,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \to c'
              is a coCartesian lift of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(c) \to d
            %p
              Therefore (by the discussion
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "final+functor#Examples"} here
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofinal+functor"} cofinal functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f_! : [C, \mathcal{D}] \to [D, \mathcal{D}]
            %p
              is equivalently given by taking the colimit over the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi d
                    %mo ↦
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi f
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi d
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f_! X : d \mapsto \lim_{\underset{f^{-1}(d)}{\to}} X
                    \\,.
          %p
            A similar result holds for
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Lurie, HTT, prop. 4.3.3.10
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo =
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S=Y
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} id
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q = \id_Y
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p The central point about examples of Kan extensions is:
          %p
            %em> Kan extensions are ubiquitous
            \.
          %p
            To a fair extent,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is all about Kan extensions and the other
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+construction"} universal construction
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
          %p
            Listing examples of Kan extensions in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is much like listing examples of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            for more).
          %p Keeping that in mind, we do list some special cases and special classes of examples that are useful to know. But any list is necessarily wildly incomplete.
          %h3#general General
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ′
                      %mo =
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C' =
                the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ran
                      %mi F
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi lim
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ran F \simeq \lim F
                and the left Kan extension is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Lan
                        %mi F
                        %mo ≃
                        %mi colim
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lan F \simeq colim F
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} morphism of sites
                coming from a functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mi t
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^t : S_Y \to S_X
                of the underlying categories, the left Kan extension of functors along
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^t
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
                operation
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo :
                        %mi PSh
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi PSh
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1} : PSh(Y) \to PSh(X)
            %li
              %p
                see also
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/examples+of+Kan+extensions"} examples of Kan extensions
          %h3#nonpointwise_kan_extensions Non-pointwise Kan extensions
          %p
            Examples of Kan extensions that are not point-wise are discussed in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Borceux"} Borceux, exercise 3.9.7
          %h3#restriction_and_extension_of_sheaves Restriction and extension of sheaves
          %p For more on the following see also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/restriction+and+extension+of+sheaves"} restriction and extension of sheaves
          %p
            The basic example for left Kan extensions using the above pointwise formula, is in the construction of the pullback of sheaves along a morphism of topological spaces. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
            be a continuous map and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            a presheaf over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f_* F)(U) = F(f^{-1}(U))
            clearly defines a presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_* F
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is. On the other hand, given a presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            we can not define pullback presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f^{-1} G)(V)=G(f(V))
            because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(V)
            might not be open in general (unless
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is an open map). For Grothendieck sites such
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(V)
            would not make even sense. But one can consider approximating from above by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(W)
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo ⊃
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W\supset f(V)
            which are open and take a colimit of this diagram of inclusions (all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            are bigger, so getting down to the lower bound means going reverse to the direction of inclusions). But inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(V)\subset W
            implies
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\subset f^{-1}(f(V))\subset f^{-1}(W)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\subset f^{-1}(W)
            involves
            = succeed "." do
              %em only open sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V\downarrow f^{-1})
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a sheaf, the colimit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(V)
            understood as a rule
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\mapsto G(V)
            is still not a sheaf, we need to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheafification"} sheafify
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} sheafify
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} colim
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ↪
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mi W
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} sheafify
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} colim
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f^{-1}G = \mathrm{sheafify}(V\mapsto\mathrm{colim}_{V\hookrightarrow f^{-1}W} G(W)) = \mathrm{sheafify}(V\mapsto\mathrm{colim}_{(V\downarrow f^{-1})} G)
          %p
            which is a sheaf, and one can analyze this construction to show that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}
            is a left adjoint to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*
          %h3#ExamplesKanExtensionsInPhysics Kan extension in physics
          %p
            We list here some occurrences of Kan extensions in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
          %p
            Notice that since, by the above discussion, Kan extensions are ubiquitous in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            and are essentially equivalent to other standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            such as notably
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} co
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory+and+physics"} category theory and physics
            at all, it necessarily also involves Kan extensions, in some guise. But here is a list of some example where they appear rather explicitly.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+quantum+field+theory"} extended quantum field theory
                on open and closed manifolds, usually the theory “in the bulk” (on closed manifolds) is induced by “extending” that “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theory"}> on the boundary
                ”, and in good cases this extension is explicitly a (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+Kan+extension"}> homotopy
                )-Kan extension. This is the case notably for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+TQFT"} 2d TQFT
                in the form of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
                (
                %a{:href => "TCFT#Costello04"}> Costello 04
                ), see at
                %em
                  %a{:href => "TCFT#Classification"} TCFT – Classification
                for details.
            %li
              %p
                When
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
                is formalized in terms of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration+in+generalized+cohomology"} fiber integration in generalized cohomology
                (as surveyed at
                = succeed ")" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} motivic quantization
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+Kan+extension"} homotopy Kan extension
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parameterized+spectrum"} parameterized spectra
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/master+thesis+Nuiten"} Nuiten 13, section 4.1
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Homotopy-type+semantics+for+quantization"} Schreiber 14, section 6.2
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ambidexterity+in+K%28n%29-Local+Stable+Homotopy+Theory"} Hopkins-Lurie 14, section 4
          %h2#remark_on_terminology_pushforward_vs_pullback Remark on terminology: pushforward vs. pullback
          %p
            Generally, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : C \to C'
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor categories
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [C', D] \stackrel{- \circ p}{\to}
                  [C,D]
          %p
            is spoken of as
            %em pulling back
            a functor on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C'
            to a functor on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            itself. For this reason, we have above denoted this functor by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            a
            %em push forward
            of functors from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to functors on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C'
          %p
            This notation also coincides with that for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphisms"} geometric morphisms
            in one case: any functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon C\to C'
            between small categories induces a geometric morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C,Set] \to [C',Set]
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+topos"} presheaf toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
            is the above
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^*
            and whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+image"} direct image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_*
            is the right Kan extension functor. Note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^*
            preserves (finite) limits, as required of an inverse image functor, since it has a left adjoint, namely
            %em left
            Kan extension.
          %p
            On the other hand, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is additionally a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+functor"} flat functor
            %em direct
            image of a geometric morphism, whose inverse image is given by
            %em left
            Kan extension (which preserves finite limits when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is flat). More generally, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C')^{op}
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sites"} sites
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mi op
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^{op}\colon C^{op}\to (C')^{op}
            is flat and preserves covering families (i.e. it is a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism+of+sites"} morphism of sites
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(C^{op})\to Sh((C')^{op})
            between sheaf toposes.
          %p
            For example,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C')^{op}
            might be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Open
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Open(X)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Open
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Open(X')
            of open subsets of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X'
            and inclusions, in which case
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Open
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Open
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Open(X)^{op} \to Open(X')^{op}
          %p come from continuous maps of topological spaces going the other way
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ←
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo :
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \leftarrow X' : f
                  \\,,
          %p
            via the usual inverse image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1} : O(X)^{op} \to O(X')^{op}
            of open subsets.
          %p
            Thus, in such cases, the functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^*
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = f^{-1}
            %em push-forward
            of (pre)sheaves along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            literature (such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+and+Sheaves"} Categories and Sheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is usually denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_*
            and not
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^*
          %p It is however noteworthy that also the opposite perspective does occur in geometrically motivated examples. For instance
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete category
                on smooth space and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo =
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = U(1)
                is the discrete category on the smooth space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                underlying the Lie group
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal to
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} smooth spaces
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : C \to D
                can be identified with smooth
                = succeed "-valued" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : C \to C'
                does correspond to the familiar notion of
                %em pullback
                of functions;
            %li
              %p
                and similar in higher degrees: if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = P_1(X)
                is the smooth path groupoid of a smooth space and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo =
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = \mathbf{B} U(1)
                the smooth
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                regarded as a one-object
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to D
                correspond to smooth 1-forms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in \Omega^1(X)
                on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : P_1(X) \to P_1(X')
                corresponds to the familiar notion of
                %em pullback
                of 1-forms.
          %p
            This means that whether or not Kan extensions correspond geometrically to pushforward or to pullback depends on the way (covariant or contravariant) in which the domain categories
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C'
            are identified with geometric entities.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong> Kan extension
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Kan+extension"} (∞,1)-Kan extension
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"}> dependent sum
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum+type"}> dependent sum type
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+type"} dependent product type
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+%28co-%29limit"} internal (co-)limit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codensity+monad"} codensity monad
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+theory"} shape theory
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The original definition is due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+M.+Kan"} Daniel M. Kan
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+M.+Kan"}> Daniel M. Kan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Adjoint functors
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/s0002-9947-1958-0131451-0"} doi:10.1090/s0002-9947-1958-0131451-0
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li#Borceux
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                , section 3.7 of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Categorical+Algebra"} Handbook of Categorical Algebra
                  I
            %li
              %p
                Kashiwara and Shapira, section 2.3 in
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+and+Sheaves"} Categories and Sheaves
          %p The book
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+Work"} Categories for the working mathematician
          %p has a famous treatment of Kan extensions with a statement: “The notion of Kan extensions subsumes all the other fundamental concepts in category theory”. Of course, many other fundamental concepts of category theory can also be regarded as subsuming all the others.
          %p
            Lecture notes with an eye towards applications in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %ul
            %li#Riehl
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"}> Emily Riehl
              , Chapter 1 in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categorical+Homotopy+Theory"} Categorical Homotopy Theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781107261457"} doi:10.1017/CBO9781107261457
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.jhu.edu/~eriehl/cathtpy.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            On Kan extensions via (co)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ends"}> ends
            \:
          %ul
            %li#Loregian21
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fosco+Loregian"}> Fosco Loregian
              , section 2 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Coend calculus
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1501.02503"} arXiv:1501.02503
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108778657"} doi:10.1017/9781108778657
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/coend-calculus/C662E90767358B336F17B606D19D8C43"} ISBN:9781108778657
          %p
            For more on Kan extensions in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eduardo+Dubuc"}> Eduardo Dubuc
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Kan extensions in enriched category theory
          %p and chapter 4 of
          %ul
            %li#Kelly
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Kelly"}> Max Kelly
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Basic Concepts of Enriched Category Theory
              %p
                Cambridge University Press, Lecture Notes in Mathematics 64, 1982, Republished in: Reprints in Theory and Applications of Categories, No. 10 (2005) pp. 1-136 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/10/tr10abs.html"}> tac:tr10
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/10/tr10.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
            notion is discussed in section 4.3 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
          %p
            For uses of Kan extension in the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+Lawvere+theory"} algebras over an algebraic theory
            see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul-Andr%C3%A9+Melli%C3%A8s"} Paul-André Melliès
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicholas+Tabareau"} Nicholas Tabareau
              %em Free models of T-algebraic theories computed as Kan extensions
              (
              %a{:href => "http://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-00339331/fr/"}> web
              )
          %p Preservation of certain limits by left Kan extended functors is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#BorceuxDay
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                , and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Day"} Brian Day
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On product-preserving Kan extension
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://journals.cambridge.org/download.php?file=%2FBAZ%2FBAZ17_02%2FS0004972700010455a.pdf&code=e8c9ebb215a55a0891f4e05f6aebbcf2"} pdf
            %li#KarazerisProtsonis
              %p
                Panagis Karazeris, Grigoris Protsonis,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Left Kan extensions preserving finite products
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.upatras.gr/~pkarazer/publications/topsift.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The general notion of extensions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-morphisms"} 1-morphisms
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-categories"} 2-categories
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              John W. Gray,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quasi-Kan extensions for 2-categories
              %a{:href => "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/346b/9ae758d63f12e8e5c635c85cb30f6c568fd9.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Lack09
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Lack"}> Steve Lack
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A 2-categories companion
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} John Baez
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+May"} Peter May
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Towards+Higher+Categories"} Towards Higher Categories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0702535"} arXiv:math/0702535
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"}> Ross Street
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Pointwise extensions and sketches in bicategories
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.6427"} arXiv:1409.6427
            %li#Roald13
              Seerp Roald Koudenburg,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algebraic weighted colimits
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1304.4079"} arXiv
          %p For the notion of (2-dimensional) (pointwise) bi-Kan extensions of pseudofunctors, see
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fernando+Lucatelli+Nunes"}> Fernando Lucatelli Nunes
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On biadjoint triangles
                %a{:href => "http://tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/31/9/31-09abs.html"} 31-9
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fernando+Lucatelli+Nunes"}> Fernando Lucatelli Nunes
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Pseudo-Kan extensions and descent theory
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/33/15/33-15abs.html"} 33-15
          %p
            and its applications to the theory of (2-dimensional)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+functors"} flat functors
            can be seen in
          %ul
            %li
              M.E. Descotte, E.J. Dubuc, M. Szyld,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the notion of flat 2-functors
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0001870818301968"} Adv. Math
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.09429"} 1610.09429
          %p For a treatment of left Kan extensions as ‘partial colimits’, see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paolo+Perrone"}> Paolo Perrone
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Walter+Tholen"} Walter Tholen
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Kan extensions are
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+evaluation"} partial
                  colimits
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.04531"} arXiv:2101.04531
        .revisedby
